15.07, "analyst_adjustment": -0.57, "key_factors": ["Well-funded incumbent: Scholten holds roughly a $1M cash edge with no outside spending against her", "Expert consensus: eight of nine ratings place the race at Safe or Solid D", "Favorable terrain: D+4 district lean plus a Democratic-friendly midterm environment", "Markets price Scholten at 93-94% over an underfunded outsider challenger"], "rationale": "Scholten combines a D+4 district, a fundraising advantage of roughly four to one, and near-unanimous Safe/Solid D ratings, while prediction markets price her win odds in the mid-nineties; DeBoer is repeatedly described as facing an uphill climb. With no public general-election polling, the margin rests on these convergent fundamentals, which point to a comfortable incumbent win.", "watch": "A sharp shift in the national midterm environment toward Republicans, or a late outside-spending push for DeBoer, could narrow the margin.", "confidence": "high", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Hillary Scholten (D) is running."]}, "overview": "Hillary Scholten, the Democratic incumbent, enters the general election as a clear favorite in a Grand Rapids-area district that leans Democratic by about four points. Terri DeBoer, a self-styled outsider who won the Republican primary, faces what local reporting describes as an uphill race, though she has sought to distinguish herself with independent positions on taxes and spending. A Green Party candidate is also on the ballot.\n\nScholten has raised roughly four times as much as DeBoer and holds a large cash advantage, with no outside groups spending against her. Nine expert ratings place the race at Safe or Solid Democratic, with one calling it Likely Democratic, and prediction markets give her a 93 to 94 percent chance. No public general-election polls are available, so the outlook rests on fundamentals, ratings, and market pricing rather than survey data."